This work introduces frameworks to improve the efficiency and reasoning performance of large language models (LLMs) through difficulty-aware training and fine-grained advantage estimation. DIET (Difficulty-Aware Training) dynamically adjusts token penalties based on task difficulty to reduce token counts while maintaining or improving reasoning performance, enabling better inference scaling and appropriate allocation of verbosity. KTAE (Key-token Advantage Estimation) addresses the coarse granularity issue in reinforcement learning algorithms by estimating fine-grained token-level advantages, leading to shorter and more accurate responses in mathematical reasoning tasks.
