This work addresses the challenge of enhancing reasoning efficiency in large language models through difficulty-aware training and fine-grained token advantage estimation. Both DIET (Difficulty-Aware Training) and KTAE improve model performance and reduce token counts while maintaining or enhancing reasoning accuracy. DIET optimizes the performance-efficiency trade-off by dynamically adjusting token penalties based on task difficulty, whereas KTAE provides a model-free method to estimate token-level advantages, leading to more concise and accurate responses in mathematical reasoning tasks.
