This work addresses the challenge of enhancing reasoning efficiency in large language models (LLMs) through difficulty-aware training and fine-grained token advantage estimation. DIET (Difficulty-Aware Training) introduces a framework that optimizes the performance-efficiency trade-off by modulating token penalties based on task difficulty, while KTAE proposes a model-free algorithm to estimate fine-grained token-level advantages in reinforcement learning settings. Both methods improve reasoning performance and reduce token counts, with DIET demonstrating superior inference scaling and more appropriate response length allocation, and KTAE achieving higher accuracy with shorter responses across mathematical reasoning benchmarks.
